{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>They have roused My jealousy with a no-god.</b> With one who has no power to save; and so it is explained in Yechezkel (8): the simurgh of jealousy (סמל הקנאה) — which means 'the provoker,' for the simurgh would provoke the Holy One, blessed be He, for He is a jealous El (אל קנא)." "<b>And I will rouse their jealousy with a no-people.</b> This is the exile of Babylon, as it is said (Isaiah 23:13): ״הן ארץ כשדים זה העם לא היה אשור״.<br> <b>With a vile nation I will provoke them.</b> This is the exile of Edom, and Scripture called Edom 'vile' (נבל), for it acted as a stranger toward its brother and stood aloof on the day of destruction; and this is what Obadiah, of blessed memory, mentioned (v. 11): ״ביום עמד מנגד ביום שבות זרים חילו״. And likewise you will find that David, of blessed memory, called it by this name in the psalm (Psalms 74:18): ״זכר זאת אויב חרף ה' ועם נבל נאצו שמך״, and he said further (ibid.): ״קומה אלהים ריבה ריבך זכור חרפתך מני נבל כל היום״, and immediately after: ״אל תתן לחית נפש תורך״, to explain that what he called עם נבל is the fourth beast that he referred to regarding them elsewhere (Psalms 68): ״גער חית קנה״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:53:21.974984448Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}